1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,923
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,923 --> 00:00:07,811
This is chapter six: lambdas and functional interfaces.

3
00:00:08,261 --> 00:00:14,272
Java 8 added lambdas, and most codebases treated them as shorter anonymous classes.

4
00:00:14,272 --> 00:00:15,792
That undersells them.

5
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:20,355
Lambdas made behaviour a value you can pass, store and compose.

6
00:00:20,355 --> 00:00:25,859
That's what streams, CompletableFuture, and the modern Map API are built on.

7
00:00:25,859 --> 00:00:27,525
Let's see how it works.

8
00:00:28,425 --> 00:00:32,145
Our order engine needs to sort cart lines by price.

9
00:00:32,145 --> 00:00:36,157
Before Java 8, you wrote an anonymous Comparator class.

10
00:00:36,157 --> 00:00:45,641
That's six lines, and the only real instruction, compare by line total, is buried inside a type, a method, and two sets of braces.

11
00:00:46,091 --> 00:00:48,206
In Java 8 it's a single line.

12
00:00:48,206 --> 00:00:52,875
Comparator dot comparing, with a method reference to line total.

13
00:00:53,325 --> 00:00:54,699
That ratio matters.

14
00:00:54,699 --> 00:01:00,051
When passing behaviour was expensive, people wrote if-else chains instead.

15
00:01:00,051 --> 00:01:05,549
Once it became cheap, passing behaviour became the normal way to write Java.

16
00:01:06,449 --> 00:01:08,012
Here's the mental model.

17
00:01:08,012 --> 00:01:15,110
A lambda is an instance of a functional interface, created from an expression instead of a class declaration.

18
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:20,360
When you assign a lambda to a Comparator of CartLine, that's its target type.

19
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:28,810
The compiler looks up that interface's single abstract method: compare, which takes two cart lines and returns an int.

20
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:36,513
The lambda's parameters and return value have to match that signature, so the compiler can infer every type for you.

21
00:01:36,963 --> 00:01:38,212
Two consequences.

22
00:01:38,212 --> 00:01:41,077
First, a lambda has no type of its own.

23
00:01:41,077 --> 00:01:46,587
The same text can be a Comparator in one place and a BiFunction in another.

24
00:01:46,587 --> 00:01:48,718
This is called target typing.

25
00:01:49,168 --> 00:01:55,174
Second, a functional interface is simply any interface with exactly one abstract method.

26
00:01:55,174 --> 00:01:57,835
Default and static methods don't count.

27
00:01:57,835 --> 00:02:04,251
That's how Comparator carries around twenty helper methods and is still a valid lambda target.

28
00:02:05,151 --> 00:02:12,761
Java ships more than forty functional interfaces, but nearly all of them are variations on four shapes.

29
00:02:12,761 --> 00:02:18,228
Function takes one value and returns one, like turning a SKU into a price.

30
00:02:18,678 --> 00:02:22,050
Predicate takes one value and returns a boolean.

31
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:23,947
Is this cart line in stock?

32
00:02:24,397 --> 00:02:27,517
Consumer takes one value and returns nothing.

33
00:02:27,517 --> 00:02:31,053
It's for side effects, like sending an order email.

34
00:02:31,503 --> 00:02:37,157
And Supplier takes nothing and returns a value, like generating a new order ID.

35
00:02:37,607 --> 00:02:40,640
Learn those four and the rest is mechanical.

36
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,364
Add Bi for two arguments.

37
00:02:42,814 --> 00:02:47,405
Add Int, Long or Double for primitive versions that avoid boxing.

38
00:02:47,405 --> 00:02:50,089
These matter in hot loops and streams.

39
00:02:50,539 --> 00:02:58,197
And UnaryOperator and BinaryOperator are just the special cases where the input and output types are the same.

40
00:02:59,097 --> 00:03:01,098
The syntax has a few forms.

41
00:03:01,098 --> 00:03:07,172
Empty parentheses for no parameters, a bare name for one, parentheses for several.

42
00:03:07,172 --> 00:03:13,543
You can add explicit types, or since Java 11, var, which lets you annotate parameters.

43
00:03:13,543 --> 00:03:17,099
An expression body returns its value implicitly.

44
00:03:17,099 --> 00:03:20,729
A block body needs braces and an explicit return.

45
00:03:21,179 --> 00:03:26,444
When a lambda just calls an existing method, use a method reference instead.

46
00:03:26,444 --> 00:03:33,233
There are four kinds: static, bound to a particular instance, unbound, and constructor references.

47
00:03:33,683 --> 00:03:37,134
The unbound kind is the one that confuses people.

48
00:03:37,134 --> 00:03:45,587
CartLine colon colon line total takes the receiver as its first parameter, so it fits a Function from CartLine to Money.

49
00:03:46,487 --> 00:03:48,519
Here's where it gets powerful.

50
00:03:48,519 --> 00:03:51,703
A pricing rule is our own functional interface.

51
00:03:51,703 --> 00:03:56,378
One abstract method, apply, which takes a subtotal and returns money.

52
00:03:56,378 --> 00:04:02,272
Its default andThen method returns a new lambda that runs this rule, then the next one.

53
00:04:02,272 --> 00:04:08,844
Mark it at FunctionalInterface, so the compiler rejects anyone who adds a second abstract method.

54
00:04:09,294 --> 00:04:19,949
Now each business rule is a one-line lambda: a ten percent bulk discount, a forty rupee delivery fee, and a hundred rupees off for the festive offer.

55
00:04:19,949 --> 00:04:23,310
Chain them with andThen into one checkout rule.

56
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:25,812
Apply it to a thousand rupees.

57
00:04:25,812 --> 00:04:33,407
The discount takes it to nine hundred, delivery adds forty, and the offer brings it to eight hundred and forty.

58
00:04:33,407 --> 00:04:43,259
The built-in interfaces work the same way: Function has andThen and compose, Predicate has and, or and negate, and Comparator has thenComparing.

59
00:04:44,159 --> 00:04:45,860
Predicates compose too.

60
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:50,371
Name each condition, in stock, perishable, then combine them.

61
00:04:50,371 --> 00:04:53,626
Needs cold chain is in stock and perishable.

62
00:04:53,626 --> 00:04:57,546
Each one is named, testable on its own, and reusable.

63
00:04:57,546 --> 00:05:00,948
An inline boolean expression is none of those.

64
00:05:01,398 --> 00:05:03,945
Comparators lose all their ceremony.

65
00:05:03,945 --> 00:05:08,403
Sort by vendor, then by product name, then by price descending.

66
00:05:08,403 --> 00:05:17,249
When the key is a number, use comparingInt, comparingLong or comparingDouble, so you don't box every key on every comparison.

67
00:05:17,699 --> 00:05:22,312
And a Supplier, or any function argument, gives you laziness.

68
00:05:22,312 --> 00:05:27,606
getOrDefault evaluates the expensive lookup every time, even on a hit.

69
00:05:27,606 --> 00:05:30,555
computeIfAbsent only runs it on a miss.

70
00:05:30,555 --> 00:05:34,488
Optional's orElseGet versus orElse is the same idea.

71
00:05:35,388 --> 00:05:36,542
Two sharp edges.

72
00:05:36,542 --> 00:05:37,625
First, capture.

73
00:05:37,625 --> 00:05:42,891
A lambda captures the value of a local variable, not the variable itself.

74
00:05:42,891 --> 00:05:48,735
So the variable must be effectively final, and reassigning it is a compile error.

75
00:05:48,735 --> 00:05:51,260
Fields don't have this restriction.

76
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:57,048
The classic workaround is a one-element array you mutate from inside the lambda.

77
00:05:57,048 --> 00:06:03,187
It compiles, but it isn't thread-safe, and it's a sign you really wanted a loop or a reduce.

78
00:06:03,637 --> 00:06:05,472
Second, checked exceptions.

79
00:06:05,472 --> 00:06:13,080
None of the standard functional interfaces declare them, so a lambda calling Files dot readString won't compile.

80
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:18,312
You can catch and rethrow as unchecked, or write your own throwing interface.

81
00:06:18,312 --> 00:06:22,048
But often the honest answer is to keep it a plain loop.

82
00:06:22,948 --> 00:06:26,566
Under the hood, a lambda is not an anonymous class.

83
00:06:26,566 --> 00:06:31,887
javac compiles the lambda body into a private method with a synthetic name.

84
00:06:32,337 --> 00:06:35,771
The call site becomes an invokedynamic instruction.

85
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:44,371
The first time it runs, the JVM calls the lambda metafactory, which spins up an implementation class at runtime.

86
00:06:44,821 --> 00:06:50,200
Then the call site is linked permanently, so later calls jump straight there.

87
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:58,303
Because the strategy is decided at runtime, lambda performance has improved across JDK versions without recompiling.

88
00:06:58,753 --> 00:07:00,380
Practical consequences.

89
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:06,177
There's no class file per lambda, so jars stay small and class loading stays fast.

90
00:07:06,627 --> 00:07:09,940
A lambda that captures nothing is a singleton.

91
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:15,630
One that captures a variable allocates a new instance each time it's evaluated.

92
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:20,750
Inside a lambda, this means the enclosing instance, not the lambda.

93
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:27,024
Remember that when converting anonymous classes, because it can silently change behaviour.

94
00:07:27,474 --> 00:07:31,411
And lambdas appear in stack traces with positional names.

95
00:07:31,411 --> 00:07:38,802
If a lambda has real logic you'll need to debug, extract it into a named method and use a method reference.

96
00:07:39,702 --> 00:07:42,299
Let's turn that into production rules.

97
00:07:42,299 --> 00:07:46,467
Do: annotate your own interfaces with at FunctionalInterface.

98
00:07:46,467 --> 00:07:50,157
Prefer method references when a lambda just delegates.

99
00:07:50,157 --> 00:07:52,070
Name non-trivial predicates.

100
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:55,213
Use the primitive specialisations for numbers.

101
00:07:55,213 --> 00:07:56,922
Compose rather than nest.

102
00:07:56,922 --> 00:08:01,910
And extract any lambda longer than about three lines into a named method.

103
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:06,746
Never: mutate captured state through arrays or holder objects.

104
00:08:06,746 --> 00:08:14,315
Never put side effects in a function that's expected to be pure, like one passed to map or computeIfAbsent.

105
00:08:14,315 --> 00:08:17,428
That's a race waiting for a parallel stream.

106
00:08:17,428 --> 00:08:20,045
Don't wrap every lambda in try catch.

107
00:08:20,045 --> 00:08:28,958
Don't overload a method on two functional interfaces with the same number of parameters, because callers hit ambiguity errors.

108
00:08:28,958 --> 00:08:31,364
And never rely on lambda identity.

109
00:08:31,364 --> 00:08:40,135
Comparing two lambdas with double equals is unspecified, which also makes them bad map keys or listeners you plan to remove.

110
00:08:41,035 --> 00:08:50,785
Java 8 introduced the whole model: lambdas, method references, the java util function package, and default and static interface methods.

111
00:08:51,235 --> 00:08:57,869
Java 9 added private interface methods, so default methods can share code without exposing it.

112
00:08:58,319 --> 00:09:08,133
Java 10 and 11 brought var, including in lambda parameters, plus Predicate dot not, which reads better than negate with a method reference.

113
00:09:08,583 --> 00:09:13,227
Records and sealed interfaces in 16 and 17 fit the model well.

114
00:09:13,227 --> 00:09:18,396
A sealed interface with one abstract method is still a lambda target.

115
00:09:18,846 --> 00:09:26,014
Java 21's pattern matching for switch replaces some old dispatch tables built from maps of functions.

116
00:09:26,464 --> 00:09:31,093
And as of Java 26, the lambda model itself is unchanged.

117
00:09:31,993 --> 00:09:32,602
To recap.

118
00:09:32,602 --> 00:09:38,624
A lambda is an instance of a functional interface, and it takes its type from its target.

119
00:09:38,624 --> 00:09:43,157
Learn the four shapes: function, predicate, consumer, and supplier.

120
00:09:43,157 --> 00:09:45,864
Compose behaviour instead of nesting it.

121
00:09:45,864 --> 00:09:50,532
And under the hood, lambdas use invokedynamic, not anonymous classes.

122
00:09:50,982 --> 00:09:57,727
The full chapter, with interview questions from foundation to architect level, is in the handbook.

123
00:09:57,727 --> 00:10:00,549
Next up is chapter seven, the Stream API.

124
00:10:00,549 --> 00:10:01,926
Thanks for watching.
